{
 "method_card": {
  "steps": [
   "Find first and second differences of the sequence",
   "If second differences are constant (d2), then a = d2/2",
   "Subtract an^2 from each term to get the linear remainder",
   "Find the nth term of the remainder to get b and c"
  ],
  "title": "How to Find Quadratic nth Terms, Use Functions and Iteration",
  "content": "<p><strong>Quadratic sequences</strong> have a constant <em>second</em> difference. The nth term has the form \\(an^2 + bn + c\\). To find it:</p><p>1) Find the second difference \\(d_2\\). Then \\(a = d_2/2\\).</p><p>2) Subtract the \\(an^2\\) sequence from the original to get a linear sequence.</p><p>3) Find the nth term of the remaining linear sequence (\\(bn + c\\)).</p><p><strong>Function notation:</strong> \\(f(x)\\) means the output when \\(x\\) is the input. \\(f(3)\\) means substitute \\(x = 3\\). Composite functions: \\(fg(x)\\) means apply \\(g\\) first, then \\(f\\). Inverse functions: \\(f^{-1}(x)\\) reverses the function.</p><p><strong>Iteration:</strong> Use a formula \\(x_{n+1} = g(x_n)\\) to generate a sequence of approximations converging to a root.</p>",
  "example": "<p><strong>Find the nth term of</strong> 3, 9, 19, 33, 51, ...</p><p><strong>First differences:</strong> 6, 10, 14, 18</p><p><strong>Second difference:</strong> 4, so \\(a = 2\\)</p><p><strong>Subtract \\(2n^2\\):</strong> 3-2=1, 9-8=1, 19-18=1, 33-32=1, 51-50=1</p><p><strong>Remainder:</strong> 1, 1, 1, 1, 1 (constant = 1, so \\(bn + c = 1\\), meaning \\(b=0, c=1\\))</p><p><strong>Answer:</strong> \\(2n^2 + 1\\)</p>"
 },
